#{[17592186496201 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496201 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496201 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496201 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496201 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496201 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496201 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496201 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186495742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496201 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e3d56696-4ed6-5583-85eb-387af7ed31be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496265 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496265 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496265 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496265 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496265 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6179c8a4-6c68-54e8-a44c-27a0976388cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496265 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=16.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-16I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-16I=[\\begin{aligned}18 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 34\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496274 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496274 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496274 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496274 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496274 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79aa6457-0ad1-5b78-8ab5-2196791a63cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496274 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496288 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496288 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140646" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496288 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496288 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496288 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9582267b-adc4-536b-9335-9619155f3b19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496288 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}37 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 52\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=64,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-64I & =[\\begin{aligned}37 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 52\\end{aligned}]-64[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-27 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-64I)x=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}37 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 52\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 27\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)x=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{16}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{15}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496292 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496292 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496292 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496292 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $U$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix\n\nII. $U$ is an orthogonal matrix\n\nIII. $Σ$ is an orthogonal matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496292 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0773915-2806-5187-a512-1daa0afdcd4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496292 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, $U$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the matrix $U$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be orthogonal.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $Σ$ must be diagonal but not necessarily orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496294 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496294 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496294 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496294 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $V?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496294 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6cc8412-6a80-579d-ba3c-43e9e90968b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496294 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $V$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
